☐ Remove any non-XML-compliant characters when generating XML-file
☐ A 'jregr add <case>' subcommand, alongside diff/suspend/ok. Consumers
  script this themselves today -- Alan carries bin/jregradd, jregrcp and
  jregrmv, which add, copy and rename the files of a case by guessing a
  fixed list of extensions. jregradd admits it in its own header: "BUG: It
  should really look in the .jregr file to know which files to add." Jregr
  is the only thing that knows what a case consists of, because it is the
  thing that parses the .jregr. Alan's slott-utf8-bom case went five years
  with its .input never committed for exactly this reason

Character sets:
✔ Jregr must never transcode -- it compares what a program emitted, so
  bytes have to pass through untouched. Explicit ISO-8859-1 at every I/O
  site, which is byte transparent because it maps 0x00-0xFF one to one.
  @done(26-09-04) Eleven sites, not the ten first counted: RegrCase's
  getPrintWriter, which writes the .output everything else compares, was
  the one missed -- and it is the very line 2681983 changed in 2018,
  moved since by a refactor
✔ XMLReporter declared encoding="ISO-8859-1" but built a default-charset
  PrintStream, so -xml emitted UTF-8 bytes claiming Latin-1 @done(26-09-04)
✔ Console: System.out follows stdout.encoding from JDK 19, so diffs were
  re-encoded on the way to the terminal. Main now sets a byte transparent
  System.out/err @done(26-09-04)
☐ Drop -Dfile.encoding=iso-8859-1 from the jregr wrapper. Verified
  redundant -- the jar behaves identically with and without it -- but kept
  a release or two in case anything depends on the old behaviour
☐ Line endings are still normalised: output is read and written a line at
  a time, so CRLF becomes LF and a missing final newline is added. That is
  what lets one .expected serve Windows and Unix, but it means the compare
  is line by line, not raw bytes. Decide whether that should stay

Crash handling:
✔ CommandRunner: keep the value of p.waitFor(), expose it as getExitValue() @done(26-09-03 10:49)
✔ RegrCase: set fatal on signal death (exit > 128 on Unix, < 0 on Windows).
  NOT on any non-zero exit -- Alan's compiler suite is full of cases that
  legitimately exit non-zero, and that would turn 362 passing tests red @done(26-09-03 10:49)
✔ RegrCase: write a diagnostic into the .output naming the signal, so a
  fatal case says why instead of being empty @done(26-09-03 10:49)
  Reads ".jregr:1: '../crash' terminated by signal 11 (SIGSEGV)"
✔ RegrCase: break out of the command loop, so later .jregr lines do not
  run after a crash @done(26-09-03 10:49)
✔ Decide the fate of crash_should_stop_execution.expected -- deleted it.
  status() short-circuits on fatal so it could not affect the result, and
  had it ever matched the .output, clean() would have deleted the .output
  that shouldStopExecutingCommandsAfterACrash reads @done(26-09-03 10:49)
☐ Windows wording: a crash there is reported as "terminated by exception
  0xC0000005", but shouldStopExecutingCommandsAfterACrash asserts on
  "terminated by signal". Only bites when the suite is run on Windows
☐ signalName() covers the signals that Linux and macOS agree on. 7 and 10
  are left out on purpose (SIGBUS/SIGUSR1 vs SIGEMT/SIGBUS) and print as a
  bare number -- resolve per-platform if that ever matters

