Build:
✔ Pin JAVA_HOME. Homebrew's ant depends on openjdk and forks JDK 26,
  while java on PATH is 21 -- so 'ant test' and a hand-run java disagree
  @done(26-09-03 12:52) 'test' now forks a JDK located off
  /usr/libexec/java_home -v ${test.jdk.version}, default 21, and echoes
  which one. Override with -Dtest.jdk=<home> or -Dtest.jdk.version=N.
  Plain 'ant test' is green; -Dtest.jdk=<a 26> still gives the 4
  RegrDirectoryRecursionTest errors, so the pin is doing the work
☐ Raise -source/-target from 1.8. Deprecated on current JDKs and due to
  be removed. Nothing requires a Java 8 compatible jar -- the java8
  commits in the log are an old upgrade, not a standing constraint
✔ Narrow the 'classpath' path id from "." to "lib" -- it currently globs
  jregr.jar itself onto the compile classpath, where a stale jar can mask
  a deleted class @done(26-09-03 12:41)
  Confirmed: with src/io/File.java deleted, the old glob compiled clean
  against the copy inside jregr.jar; it now fails loudly
✔ A stale bin/ silently tests old code: javac's up-to-date check compares
  mtimes, and a fresh checkout can have sources older than old classes
  @done(26-09-03 12:41) compile and compile-tests now empty bin/ and
  test-bin/ first. Confirmed: a backdated Main.java was skipped by javac
  entirely, and bare 'ant' shipped a jar reporting the old version
✔ Release process for jregr.jar. It sat at 0.2.4 for years while the
  source said 0.2.5, and ~/bin/jregr was pointing straight at it
  @done(26-09-03 12:58) 'ant release' is now the one command: clean, then
  test, then package, then run the jar with -version so the release states
  what it actually contains. Bare 'ant' is 'build' and tests too, so no
  route to a jar skips the suite except asking for create_run_jar by name.
  Verified that a failing test produces no jar.
  Bumping JREGR_VERSION in Main.java is still a manual step beforehand,
  and ~/bin/jregr is still outside the repo's reach

